         <t>If no applicable specification, profile, agreement, commitment, or policy called for a Gateway record for R, that record was <strong>Not Expected</strong>. Non-participation in a particular audit mechanism does not, by itself, establish that no production expectation applied. The available evidence can still be insufficient for the full Audit Task.</t>
         <t>If the available information does not establish whether an Evidence Production Expectation applied to R, the state is <strong>Expectation Undetermined</strong>. Uncertainty about effective coverage does not, by itself, make a known production expectation undetermined.</t>
         <t>A further case illustrates <strong>Expected but Search Incomplete</strong> under the applicable production expectation. Suppose the gateway slot is empty, one declared source has been searched completely, and another declared source is unavailable. The auditor can report that no qualifying record was found in the first source for the declared query and cutoff. The cross-source audit remains incomplete or Unresolved; it does not support a bounded-absence finding over all declared sources. A bounded-absence finding requires the applicable search to be complete over all declared sources for the declared query and cutoff. This distinction follows the proposed conformance cases in <xref target="IMAN-CASES"/>.</t>
+        <t>For an Audit Store query interface, an empty set of returned records alone does not distinguish this incomplete search from a completed search with no qualifying records across both declared sources. Interpreting the response depends on preserving that distinction (see <xref target="collection-and-query-interfaces"/>).</t>
         <t>This example shows why evidence requirements, production expectations, and coverage cannot be collapsed into one concept.</t>
       </section>
